<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kissi people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kissi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kissi are a West African people living where the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and Liberia meet, in Liberia mainly in Lofa County. They speak a Mel language, are known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers, and are noted for the small stone figures, called pomdo, found in their fields.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kissi-people.jpg" alt="Kissi people" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orchestre_d%27un_chef_du_pays_de_Kissi_(Guin%C3%A9e)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Kissi tradition, as recorded in The Peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the Kissi lived in the Upper Niger region before the seventeenth century, south of the Futa Jallon, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalunka">Yalunka</a> people drove them out. After 1600 they moved westward, expelling the Limbas as they went, while living under constant threat from the Kurankos. They are also known by many other names, among them Assi, Gissi, Gizi, Kisi and Kizi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> the warrior Kissi Kaba Keita united many Kissi chiefdoms under his rule and resisted French conquest. Before the French attacks he had rallied the Kurankos of Morige and the Leles of Yombiro. When the French arrived in 1892 he left the largely autonomous local chiefs to defend their own areas and fought mainly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics, which delayed the conquest. By 1893 he judged that resistance would fail and submitted, and the French recognised him as chief of the northern Kissi territory. Relations worsened, the French appointed his rivals in several of his chiefdoms, and he was eventually executed at Siguiri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kissi are the fourth largest ethnic group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, about 6.2 per cent of its population, and also live in Liberia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. Their language belongs to the Mel branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>–Congo family. They are known for making baskets and weaving on vertical looms, and in the past their smiths, working the rich iron deposits of the region, produced the Kissi penny.</p><p>Many Kissi have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, but most continue to practise their traditional religion, in which ancestor worship and prayer to deceased relatives are common. Before colonial contact they carved many soapstone figures and heads. Their purpose is unclear: some scholars link them to ancestor worship, others think they may represent gods meant to increase harvests. A large number are held in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kissi people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liberian society was long divided between the Americo-Liberian settlers and their descendants, a few per cent of the population concentrated in coastal towns, and the indigenous majority, including the Kpelle, Bassa, Grebo, Kru, Vai, Gola, <b>Kissi</b>, Loma, Mano, Dan and Mandinka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
